## an6.16
an6.16:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Bhaggas at Crocodile’s Bellow, in the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood. | T: Once the Blessed One was staying among the Bhaggas in the Deer Park at Bhesakaḷā Forest, near Crocodile Haunt.
an6.16:1.2 | S: Now at that time the householder Nakula’s father was sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: At that time, Nakula’s father [Nakulapitar], the householder, was diseased, in pain, severely ill.
an6.16:1.3 | S: Then the housewife Nakula’s mother said to him: | T: Then Nakula’s mother [Nakulamatar] said to him:
an6.16:2.1 | S: “Householder, don’t pass away with concerns. | T: “Don’t be worried as you die, householder.
an6.16:2.2 | S: Such concern is suffering, | T: Death is painful for one who is worried.
an6.16:2.3 | S: and it’s criticized by the Buddha. | T: The Blessed One has criticized being worried at the time of death.
an6.16:2.4 | S: Householder, you might think: | T: “Now it may be that you are thinking,
an6.16:2.5 | S: ‘When I’ve gone, the housewife Nakula’s mother won’t be able to provide for the children and keep up the household carpets.’ | T: ‘Nakula’s mother will not be able to support the children or maintain the household after I’m gone,’
an6.16:2.6 | S: But you should not see it like this. | T: but you shouldn’t see things in that way.
an6.16:2.7 | S: I’m skilled at spinning cotton and carding wool. | T: I am skilled at spinning cotton, at carding matted wool.
an6.16:2.8 | S: I’m able to provide for the children and keep up the household carpets. | T: I can support the children and maintain the household after you are gone.
an6.16:2.9 | S: So householder, don’t pass away with concerns … | T: So don’t be worried as you die, householder.
an6.16:2.10 | P: Dukkhā, gahapati, sāpekkhassa kālakiriyā; | T: Death is painful for one who is worried.
an6.16:2.11 | P: garahitā ca bhagavatā sāpekkhassa kālakiriyā. | T: The Blessed One has criticized being worried at the time of death.
