## an6.17
an6.17:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta's Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
an6.17:1.2 | S: Then in the late afternoon, the Buddha came out of retreat, went to the assembly hall, and sat down on the seat spread out. | T: Then, in the evening, having risen from seclusion, he went to the meeting hall and, on arrival, sat down on a seat made ready.
an6.17:1.3 | S: Venerable Sāriputta also came out of retreat, went to the assembly hall, bowed to the Buddha and sat down to one side. | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta, in the evening, having risen from seclusion, also went to the meeting hall and, on arrival, after bowing down to the Blessed One, sat to one side.
an6.17:1.4 | S: Venerables Mahāmoggallāna, | T: Then Ven. Mahā Moggallāna…
an6.17:1.5 | S: Mahākassapa, | T: Ven. Mahā Kassapa…
an6.17:1.6 | S: Mahākaccāna, | T: Ven. Mahā Kaccāna…
an6.17:1.7 | S: Mahākoṭṭhita, | T: Ven. Mahā Koṭṭhita…
an6.17:1.8 | S: Mahācunda, | T: Ven. Mahā Cunda…
an6.17:1.9 | S: Mahākappina, | T: Ven. Mahā Kappina…
an6.17:1.10 | S: Anuruddha, | T: Ven. Anuruddha…
an6.17:1.11 | S: Revata, | T: Ven. Revata…
an6.17:1.12 | S: and Ānanda did the same. | T: Ven. Ānanda, in the evening, having risen from seclusion, also went to the meeting hall and, on arrival, after bowing down to the Blessed One, sat to one side.
an6.17:1.13 | S: The Buddha spent much of the night sitting in meditation, then rose from his seat and entered his dwelling. | T: Then the Blessed One, having spent most of the night sitting, got up from his seat and entered his dwelling.
an6.17:1.14 | S: And soon after the Buddha left those venerables each went to their own dwelling. | T: Not long after he had left, those venerable ones also got up from their seats and went to their own dwellings.
an6.17:1.15 | S: But those mendicants who were junior, recently gone forth, newly come to this teaching and training slept until the sun came up, snoring. | T: But those monks who were new—not long gone forth, recently come to this Dhamma & Vinaya—slept until sunrise, snoring.
an6.17:1.16 | S: The Buddha saw them doing this, with his clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman. | T: The Blessed One, with his divine eye, purified & surpassing the human—saw those monks sleeping until sunrise, snoring.
an6.17:1.17 | S: He went to the assembly hall, sat down on the seat spread out, | T: Seeing them, he went to the meeting hall and sat down on a seat made ready.
an6.17:1.18 | S: and addressed the mendicants: | T: Seated, he said to the monks,
an6.17:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, where is Sāriputta? | T: “Monks, where is Sāriputta?
an6.17:2.2 | S: Where are Mahāmoggallāna, | T: Where is Mahā Moggallāna?
an6.17:2.3 | S: Mahākassapa, | T: Where is Mahā Kassapa?
an6.17:2.4 | S: Mahākaccāna, | T: Where is Mahā Kaccāna?
an6.17:2.5 | S: Mahākoṭṭhita, | T: Where is Mahā Koṭṭhita?
an6.17:2.6 | S: Mahācunda, | T: Where is Mahā Cunda?
an6.17:2.7 | S: Mahākappina, | T: Where is Mahā Kappina?
an6.17:2.8 | S: Anuruddha, | T: Where is Anuruddha?
an6.17:2.9 | S: Revata, | T: Where is Revata?
an6.17:2.10 | S: and Ānanda? | T: Where is Ānanda?
an6.17:2.11 | S: Where have these senior disciples gone?” | T: Where have those elder disciples gone?”
an6.17:2.12 | S: “Soon after the Buddha left those venerables each went to their own dwelling.” | T: “Lord, not long after the Blessed One left, those venerable ones left and went to their own dwellings.”
an6.17:2.13 | S: “So, mendicants, when the senior mendicants left, why did you sleep until the sun came up, snoring? | T: “But why, when those elder monks were gone, did you sleep until sunrise, snoring?
an6.17:2.14 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: “What do you think, monks:
an6.17:2.15 | S: Have you ever seen or heard | T: Have you ever seen or heard
an6.17:2.16 | S: of an anointed aristocratic king who rules his whole life, dear and beloved to the country, while indulging in the pleasures of sleeping, lying down, and drowsing as much as he likes?” | T: of a head-anointed noble warrior king who—spending as much time as he likes committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of reclining, the pleasure of torpor—maintains his rule all his life or is pleasing & agreeable to the country?”
an6.17:2.17 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
an6.17:2.18 | S: “Good, mendicants! | T: “Good, monks.
an6.17:2.19 | S: I too have never seen or heard of such a thing. | T: I, too, have never seen or heard of
an6.17:2.20 | P: ‘rājā khattiyo muddhāvasitto yāvadatthaṁ seyyasukhaṁ passasukhaṁ middhasukhaṁ anuyutto viharanto yāvajīvaṁ rajjaṁ kārento janapadassa vā piyo manāpo’ti. | T: a head-anointed noble warrior king who—spending as much time as he likes committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of reclining, the pleasure of torpor—maintains his rule all his life or is pleasing & agreeable to the country.
an6.17:3.1 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: “What do you think, monks:
an6.17:3.2 | S: Have you ever seen or heard | T: Have you ever seen or heard
an6.17:3.3 | S: of an appointed official … | T: of a royal minister…
an6.17:3.4 | S: a hereditary official … | T: a royal heir…
an6.17:3.5 | S: a general … | T: a general…
an6.17:3.6 | S: a village chief … | T: a village headman…
an6.17:3.7 | S: or a guild head who runs the guild his whole life, dear and beloved to the guild, while indulging in the pleasures of sleeping, lying down, and drowsing as much as he likes?” | T: a guild master who— spending as much time as he likes committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of reclining, the pleasure of torpor—maintains leadership over his guild all his life or is pleasing & agreeable to the guild?”
an6.17:3.8 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
an6.17:3.9 | S: “Good, mendicants! | T: “Good, monks.
an6.17:3.10 | S: I too have never seen or heard of such a thing. | T: I, too, have never seen or heard of
an6.17:3.11 | P: ‘pūgagāmaṇiko yāvadatthaṁ seyyasukhaṁ passasukhaṁ middhasukhaṁ anuyutto viharanto yāvajīvaṁ pūgagāmaṇikattaṁ vā kārento pūgassa vā piyo manāpo’ti. | T: a guild master who— spending as much time as he likes committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of reclining, the pleasure of torpor—maintains leadership over his guild all his life or is pleasing & agreeable to the guild.
an6.17:4.1 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: “What do you think, monks:
an6.17:4.2 | S: Have you ever seen or heard | T: Have you ever seen or heard
an6.17:4.3 | S: of an ascetic or brahmin who indulges in the pleasures of sleeping, lying down, and drowsing as much as they like? Their sense doors are unguarded, they eat too much, they’re not dedicated to wakefulness, they’re unable to discern skillful qualities, and they don’t pursue the development of the qualities on the side of awakening in the evening and toward dawn. Yet they realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements.” | T: of a contemplative or brahman who—spending as much time as he likes committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of reclining, the pleasure of torpor, with his senses unguarded, knowing no moderation in food, not committed to wakefulness, with no insight into skillful qualities, not committed to the development of the wings to awakening in the early & late hours of the night—through the ending of the effluents, has entered & is remaining in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing them for himself right in the here-&-now?”
an6.17:4.4 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
an6.17:4.5 | S: “Good, mendicants! | T: “Good, monks.
an6.17:4.6 | S: I too have never seen or heard of such a thing. | T: I, too, have never seen or heard of
an6.17:4.7 | P: ‘samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā yāvadatthaṁ seyyasukhaṁ passasukhaṁ middhasukhaṁ anuyutto indriyesu aguttadvāro bhojane amattaññū jāgariyaṁ ananuyutto avipassako kusalānaṁ dhammānaṁ pubbarattāpararattaṁ bodhipakkhiyānaṁ dhammānaṁ bhāvanānuyogaṁ ananuyutto āsavānaṁ khayā anāsavaṁ cetovimuttiṁ paññāvimuttiṁ diṭṭheva dhamme sayaṁ abhiññā sacchikatvā upasampajja viharanto’ti. | T: a contemplative or brahman who—spending as much time as he likes committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of reclining, the pleasure of torpor, with his senses unguarded, knowing no moderation in food, not committed to wakefulness, with no insight into skillful qualities, not committed to the development of the wings to awakening in the early & late hours of the night—through the ending of the effluents, has entered & is remaining in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing them for himself right in the here-&-now.
an6.17:5.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Therefore, monks, you should train yourselves:
an6.17:5.2 | S: ‘We will guard our sense doors, eat in moderation, be dedicated to wakefulness, discern skillful qualities, and pursue the development of the qualities on the side of awakening in the evening and toward dawn.’ | T: ‘We will have our senses guarded, know moderation in food, commit to wakefulness, gain insight into skillful qualities, and dwell committed to the development of the wings to awakening in the early & late hours of the night.’
an6.17:5.3 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.”
an6.17:5.4 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.20
an6.20:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying at Ñātika in the brick house. | T: I have heard that at one time the Blessed One was staying near Nādika in the Brick Hall.
an6.20:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks,
an6.20:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, when mindfulness of death is developed and cultivated it’s very fruitful and beneficial. It has freedom from death as its objective and culmination. | T: “Monks, mindfulness of death—when developed & pursued—is of great fruit & great benefit. It gains a footing in the deathless, has the deathless as its final end.

