an6.20:1.4 | S: And how is mindfulness of death developed and cultivated to be very fruitful and beneficial, with freedom from death as its objective and culmination? | T: And how is mindfulness of death developed & pursued so that it is of great fruit & great benefit, gains a footing in the deathless, and has the deathless as its final end?
an6.20:2.1 | S: As day passes by and night draws close, a mendicant reflects: | T: “There is the case where a monk, as day departs and night returns, reflects:
an6.20:2.2 | S: ‘I might die of many causes. | T: ‘Many are the (possible) causes of my death.
an6.20:2.3 | S: A snake might bite me, or a scorpion or centipede might sting me. | T: A snake might bite me, a scorpion might sting me, a centipede might bite me.
an6.20:2.4 | S: And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress. | T: That would be how my death would come about. That would be an obstruction for me.
an6.20:2.5 | S: Or I might stumble off a cliff, or get food poisoning, or suffer a disturbance of bile, phlegm, or piercing winds. | T: Stumbling, I might fall; my food, digested, might trouble me; my bile might be provoked, my phlegm… piercing wind forces (in the body) might be provoked.
an6.20:2.6 | S: And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress.’ | T: That would be how my death would come about. That would be an obstruction for me.’
an6.20:2.7 | S: That mendicant should reflect: | T: Then the monk should investigate:
an6.20:2.8 | S: ‘Are there any bad, unskillful qualities that I haven’t given up, which might be an obstacle to my progress if I die tonight?’ | T: ‘Are there any evil, unskillful qualities unabandoned by me that would be an obstruction for me were I to die in the night?’
an6.20:3.1 | S: Suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows that | T: If, on reflecting, he realizes that
an6.20:3.2 | S: there are such bad, unskillful qualities. Then in order to give them up they should apply extraordinary enthusiasm, effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness. | T: there are evil, unskillful qualities unabandoned by him that would be an obstruction for him were he to die in the night, then he should put forth extra desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness for the abandoning of those very same evil, unskillful qualities.
an6.20:3.3 | S: Suppose your clothes or head were on fire. In order to extinguish it, you’d apply extraordinary enthusiasm, effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness. | T: Just as when a person whose turban or head was on fire would put forth extra desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness to put out the fire on his turban or head,
an6.20:3.4 | S: In the same way, in order to give up those bad, unskillful qualities, that mendicant should apply extraordinary enthusiasm … | T: in the same way the monk should put forth extra desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness for the abandoning of those very same evil, unskillful qualities.
an6.20:4.1 | S: But suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows that | T: But if, on reflecting, he realizes that
an6.20:4.2 | S: there are no such bad, unskillful qualities. Then that mendicant should meditate with rapture and joy, training day and night in skillful qualities. | T: there are no evil, unskillful qualities unabandoned by him that would be an obstruction for him were he to die in the night, then for that very reason he should dwell in joy & rapture, training himself day & night in skillful qualities.
an6.20:5.1 | S: Or else, as night passes by and day draws close, a mendicant reflects: | T: “Further, there is the case where a monk, as night departs and day returns, reflects:
an6.20:5.2 | S: ‘I might die of many causes. | T: ’Many are the (possible) causes of my death.
an6.20:5.3 | S: A snake might bite me, or a scorpion or centipede might sting me. | T: A snake might bite me, a scorpion might sting me, a centipede might bite me.
an6.20:5.4 | S: And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress. | T: That would be how my death would come about. That would be an obstruction for me.
an6.20:5.5 | S: Or I might stumble off a cliff, or get food poisoning, or suffer a disturbance of bile, phlegm, or piercing winds. | T: Stumbling, I might fall; my food, digested, might trouble me; my bile might be provoked, my phlegm… piercing wind forces (in the body) might be provoked.
an6.20:5.6 | S: And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress.’ | T: That would be how my death would come about. That would be an obstruction for me.’
an6.20:5.7 | S: That mendicant should reflect: | T: Then the monk should investigate:
an6.20:5.8 | S: ‘Are there any bad, unskillful qualities that I haven’t given up, which might be an obstacle to my progress if I die today?’ | T: ‘Are there any evil, unskillful qualities unabandoned by me that would be an obstruction for me were I to die during the day?’
an6.20:6.1 | S: Suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows that | T: If, on reflecting, he realizes that
an6.20:6.2 | S: there are such bad, unskillful qualities. Then in order to give them up they should apply extraordinary enthusiasm, effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness. | T: there are evil, unskillful qualities unabandoned by him that would be an obstruction for him were he to die during the day, then he should put forth extra desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness for the abandoning of those very same evil, unskillful qualities.
an6.20:6.3 | S: Suppose your clothes or head were on fire. In order to extinguish it, you’d apply extraordinary enthusiasm, effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness. | T: Just as when a person whose turban or head was on fire would put forth extra desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness to put out the fire on his turban or head,
an6.20:6.4 | S: In the same way, in order to give up those bad, unskillful qualities, that mendicant should apply extraordinary enthusiasm … | T: in the same way the monk should put forth extra desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness for the abandoning of those very same evil, unskillful qualities.
an6.20:7.1 | S: But suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows that | T: But if, on reflecting, he realizes that
an6.20:7.2 | S: there are no such bad, unskillful qualities. Then that mendicant should meditate with rapture and joy, training day and night in skillful qualities. | T: there are no evil, unskillful qualities unabandoned by him that would be an obstruction for him were he to die during the day, then for that very reason he should dwell in joy & rapture, training himself day & night in skillful qualities.
an6.20:8.1 | S: Mindfulness of death, when developed and cultivated in this way, is very fruitful and beneficial. It has freedom from death as its objective and culmination.” | T: “This, monks, is how mindfulness of death is developed & pursued so that it is of great fruit & great benefit, gains a footing in the Deathless, and has the Deathless as its final end.” That is what the Blessed One said. Gratified, the monks delighted in the Blessed One’s words.
an6.20:8.2 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an6.20:8.3 | P: Sāraṇīyavaggo dutiyo. | T: (none)
