## an6.31
an6.31:1.1 | S: “These six things lead to the decline of a mendicant trainee. | T: “Monks, six qualities lead to the decline of a monk in training.
an6.31:1.2 | S: What six? | T: Which six?
an6.31:1.3 | S: They relish work, talk, sleep, and company. They don’t guard the sense doors, and they eat too much. | T: Delighting in work, delighting in talking, delighting in sleeping, delighting in entanglement, not guarding the sense faculties, not knowing moderation in food.
an6.31:1.4 | S: These six things lead to the decline of a mendicant trainee. | T: These six qualities lead to the decline of a monk in training.
an6.31:2.1 | S: These six things don’t lead to the decline of a mendicant trainee. | T: “Monks, six qualities lead to the non-decline of a monk in training.
an6.31:2.2 | S: What six? | T: Which six?
an6.31:2.3 | S: They don’t relish work, talk, sleep, and company. They guard the sense doors, and they don’t eat too much. | T: Not delighting in work, not delighting in talking, not delighting in sleeping, not delighting in entanglement, guarding the sense faculties, and knowing moderation in food.
an6.31:2.4 | S: These six things don’t lead to the decline of a mendicant trainee.” | T: These six qualities lead to the non-decline of a monk in training.”
an6.31:2.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.37
an6.37:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
an6.37:1.2 | S: Now at that time Veḷukaṇṭakī, Nanda’s mother, was preparing a religious donation with six factors for the mendicant Saṅgha headed by Sāriputta and Moggallāna. | T: And on that occasion the female lay follower Veḷukaṇṭakin Nandamātar, had established a donation endowed with six factors for the Saṅgha of monks headed by Sāriputta & Moggallāna.
an6.37:1.3 | S: The Buddha saw her doing this, with his clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, | T: The Blessed One saw with his divine eye, surpassing the human, that the laywoman Veḷukaṇḍakī, Nanda’s mother, had established a donation endowed with six factors for the Saṅgha of monks headed by Sāriputta & Moggallāna.
an6.37:1.4 | S: and he addressed the mendicants: | T: On seeing this, he addressed the monks:
an6.37:1.5 | S: “This Veḷukaṇṭakī, Nanda’s mother, is preparing a religious donation with six factors for the mendicant Saṅgha headed by Sāriputta and Moggallāna. | T: “Monks, the female lay follower Veḷukaṇṭakin Nandamātar, has established a donation endowed with six factors for the Saṅgha of monks headed by Sāriputta & Moggallāna.
an6.37:2.1 | S: And how does a religious donation have six factors? | T: “And how is a donation endowed with six factors?
an6.37:2.2 | S: Three factors apply to the donor and three to the recipients. | T: There is the case where there are the three factors of the donor, the three factors of the recipients.
an6.37:2.3 | S: What three factors apply to the donor? | T: “And which are the three factors of the donor?
an6.37:2.4 | S: It’s when a donor is in a good mood before giving, while giving they feel confident, and after giving they’re uplifted. | T: There is the case where the donor, before giving, is glad; while giving, his/her mind is bright & clear; and after giving is gratified.
an6.37:2.5 | S: These three factors apply to the donor. | T: These are the three factors of the donor.
an6.37:3.1 | S: What three factors apply to the recipients? | T: “And which are the three factors of the recipients?
an6.37:3.2 | S: It’s when the recipients are free of greed, hate, and delusion, or practicing to be free of them. | T: There is the case where the recipients are free of passion or are practicing for the subduing of passion; free of aversion or practicing for the subduing of aversion; and free of delusion or practicing for the subduing of delusion.
an6.37:3.3 | S: These three factors apply to the recipients. | T: These are the three factors of the recipients.
an6.37:3.4 | S: Thus three factors apply to the donor and three to the recipients. | T: “Such are the three factors of the donor, the three factors of the recipients.
an6.37:3.5 | S: That’s how a religious donation has six factors. | T: And this is how a donation is endowed with six factors.
an6.37:4.1 | S: It’s not easy to grasp the merit of such a six-factored donation by saying that | T: “And it’s not easy to take the measure of the merit of a donation thus endowed with six factors as
an6.37:4.2 | S: this is the extent of their overflowing merit, overflowing goodness that nurtures happiness and is conducive to heaven, ripening in happiness and leading to heaven. And it leads to what is likable, desirable, agreeable, to welfare and happiness. | T: ‘just this much a bonanza of merit, a bonanza of what is skillful—a nutriment of bliss, heavenly, resulting in bliss, leading to heaven—that leads to what is desirable, pleasing, charming, beneficial, pleasant.’
an6.37:4.3 | S: It’s simply reckoned as an incalculable, immeasurable, great mass of merit. | T: It is simply reckoned as a great mass of merit, incalculable, immeasurable.
an6.37:5.1 | S: It’s like trying to grasp how much water is in the ocean. It’s not easy to say | T: “Just as it’s not easy to take the measure of the great ocean as
an6.37:5.2 | S: how many gallons, how many hundreds, thousands, hundreds of thousands of gallons there are. | T: ‘just this many buckets of water, just this many hundreds of buckets of water, just this many thousands of buckets of water, or just this many hundreds of thousands of buckets of water.’
an6.37:5.3 | S: It’s simply reckoned as an incalculable, immeasurable, great mass of water. | T: It’s simply reckoned as a great mass of water, incalculable, immeasurable.
an6.37:5.4 | S: In the same way, it’s not easy to grasp the merit of such a six-factored donation … | T: In the same way, it’s not easy to take the measure of the merit of a donation thus endowed with six factors as
an6.37:5.5 | P: ‘ettako puññābhisando kusalābhisando sukhassāhāro sovaggiko sukhavipāko saggasaṁvattaniko iṭṭhāya kantāya manāpāya hitāya sukhāya saṁvattatī’ti. | T: ‘just this much a bonanza of merit, a bonanza of what is skillful—a nutriment of bliss, heavenly, resulting in bliss, leading to heaven—that leads to what is desirable, pleasing, charming, beneficial, pleasant.’
an6.37:5.6 | P: Atha kho asaṅkhyeyyo appameyyo mahāpuññakkhandhotveva saṅkhaṁ gacchatī”ti. | T: It is simply reckoned as a great mass of merit, incalculable, immeasurable.”
an6.37:6.1 | S: A good mood before giving, | T: Before giving, glad;
an6.37:6.2 | S: confidence while giving, | T: while giving, the mind is bright & clear;
an6.37:6.3 | S: feeling uplifted after giving: | T: having given, one is gratified:
an6.37:6.4 | S: this is the perfect sacrifice. | T: This is the consummation of the sacrifice.
an6.37:7.1 | S: Free of greed, free of hate, | T: Free of passion, free of aversion,
an6.37:7.2 | S: free of delusion, undefiled; | T: free of delusion, without effluent:
an6.37:7.3 | S: this is the field for the perfect sacrifice, | T: the consummation of the field of the sacrifice,
an6.37:7.4 | S: the disciplined spiritual practitioners. | T: one restrained, leading the holy life.
an6.37:8.1 | S: After rinsing, | T: Having rinsed oneself,
an6.37:8.2 | S: you give with your own hands. | T: having given with one’s own hands,
an6.37:8.3 | S: This sacrifice is very fruitful | T: then—
an6.37:8.4 | S: for both yourself and others. | T: because of oneself, because of the other— that is a sacrifice yielding great fruit.
an6.37:9.1 | S: When an intelligent, faithful person, | T: Having given thus —intelligent—
an6.37:9.2 | S: sacrifices like this, with a mind of letting go, | T: a person of conviction, with awareness released,
an6.37:9.3 | S: that astute one is reborn | T: reappears —wise—
an6.37:9.4 | S: in a happy, pleasing world.” | T: in a world of bliss unalloyed.
an6.37:9.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.41
an6.41:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an6.41:1.2 | S: At one time Venerable Sāriputta was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha on Vulture Peak Mountain.
an6.41:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Sāriputta robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, descended the Vulture’s Peak together with several mendicants. At a certain spot he saw a large tree trunk, | T: Then early in the morning, Ven. Sāriputta, having adjusted his lower robe and carrying his bowl & outer robe, was coming down from Vulture Peak Mountain with a large group of monks when he saw a large wood pile off to one side.
an6.41:1.4 | S: and he addressed the mendicants, | T: Seeing it, he said to the monks,
an6.41:1.5 | S: “Reverends, do you see this large tree trunk?” | T: “Friends, do you see that large wood pile over there?”
an6.41:1.6 | S: “Yes, reverend.” | T: “Yes, friend,” the monks replied.
an6.41:2.1 | S: “If they wanted to, a mendicant with psychic powers who has mastered their mind could determine this tree trunk to be nothing but earth. | T: “Friends, if he wanted to, a monk with psychic power, having attained mastery of his mind, could will that wood pile to be nothing but earth.
an6.41:2.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an6.41:2.3 | S: Because the earth element exists in the tree trunk. Relying on that a mendicant with psychic powers could determine it to be nothing but earth. | T: There is earth-property [or: earth-potential] in that wood pile, in dependence on which he could will that wood pile to be nothing but earth.
an6.41:2.4 | S: If they wanted to, a mendicant with psychic powers who has mastered their mind could determine this tree trunk to be nothing but water. … | T: “If he wanted to, a monk with psychic power, having attained mastery of his mind, could will that wood pile to be nothing but water…
an6.41:2.5 | S: Or they could determine it to be nothing but fire … | T: fire…
an6.41:2.6 | S: Or they could determine it to be nothing but air … | T: wind…
an6.41:2.7 | S: Or they could determine it to be nothing but beautiful … | T: beautiful…
an6.41:2.8 | S: Or they could determine it to be nothing but ugly. | T: unattractive.
an6.41:2.9 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an6.41:2.10 | S: Because the element of ugliness exists in the tree trunk. Relying on that a mendicant with psychic powers could determine it to be nothing but ugly.” | T: There is the property of the unattractive in that wood pile, in dependence on which he could will that wood pile to be nothing but unattractive.”
an6.41:2.11 | P: Ekādasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.42
an6.42:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an6.42:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants when he arrived at a village of the Kosalan brahmins named Icchānaṅgala. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One, on a wandering tour among the Kosalans with a large Saṅgha of monks, arrived at a Kosalan brahman village named Icchānaṅgala.
an6.42:1.3 | S: He stayed in a forest near Icchānaṅgala. | T: There he stayed in the Icchānaṅgala forest grove.
an6.42:1.4 | S: The brahmins and householders of Icchānaṅgala heard: | T: The brahman householders of Icchānaṅgala heard it said,
an6.42:1.5 | S: “It seems the ascetic Gotama—a Sakyan, gone forth from a Sakyan family—has arrived at Icchānaṅgala. He is staying in a forest near Icchānaṅgala. | T: “Gotama the contemplative—the son of the Sakyans, having gone forth from the Sakyan clan—on a wandering tour among the Kosalans with a large Saṅgha of monks—has arrived at Icchānaṅgala and is staying in the Icchānaṅgala forest grove.
an6.42:1.6 | S: He has this good reputation: | T: And of that Master Gotama this fine reputation has spread:
an6.42:1.7 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘He is indeed a Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.
an6.42:1.8 | S: He has realized with his own insight this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—and he makes it known to others. He teaches Dhamma that’s good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased. And he reveals a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. It’s good to see such perfected ones.” | T: He makes known—having realized it through direct knowledge—this world with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk; he explains the Dhamma admirable in the beginning, admirable in the middle, admirable in the end; he expounds the holy life both in its particulars & in its essence, entirely perfect, surpassingly pure. It is good to see such a worthy one.’”
an6.42:1.9 | S: Then, when the night had passed, they took abundant fresh and cooked foods and went to the forest near Icchānaṅgala, where they stood outside the gates making a colossal racket. | T: So the brahman householders of Icchānaṅgala, when the night was gone, taking many staple & non-staple foods, went to the gate house of the Icchānaṅgala forest grove. On arrival they stood there making a loud racket, a great racket.
an6.42:2.1 | S: Now, at that time Venerable Nāgita was the Buddha’s attendant. | T: Now at that time Ven. Nāgita was the Blessed One’s attendant.
an6.42:2.2 | S: Then the Buddha said to Nāgita, | T: So the Blessed One addressed Ven. Nāgita:
an6.42:2.3 | S: “Nāgita, who’s making that colossal racket? You’d think it was fishermen hauling in a catch!” | T: “Nāgita, what is that loud racket, that great racket, like fishermen with a catch of fish?”
an6.42:2.4 | S: “Sir, it’s these brahmins and householders of Icchānaṅgala. They’ve brought many fresh and cooked foods, and they’re standing outside the gates wanting to offer it specially to the Buddha and the mendicant Saṅgha.” | T: “Lord, those are the brahman householders of Icchānaṅgala standing at the gate house to the Icchānaṅgala forest grove, having brought many staple & non-staple foods for the sake of the Blessed One & the Saṅgha of monks.”
an6.42:2.5 | S: “Nāgita, may I never become famous. May fame not come to me. | T: “May I have nothing to do with honor, Nāgita, and honor nothing to do with me.
an6.42:2.6 | S: There are those who can’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: Whoever cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening,
an6.42:2.7 | S: Let them enjoy the filthy, lazy pleasure of possessions, honor, and popularity.” | T: let him consent to this slimy-excrement-pleasure, this torpor-pleasure, this pleasure of gains, offerings, & fame.”
an6.42:3.1 | S: “Sir, may the Blessed One please relent now! May the Holy One relent! | T: “Lord, let the Blessed One acquiesce (to their offerings) now! Let the One Well-Gone acquiesce now!
an6.42:3.2 | S: Now is the time for the Buddha to relent. | T: Now is the time for the Blessed One’s acquiescence, lord! Now is the time for the Blessed One’s acquiescence, lord!
an6.42:3.3 | S: Wherever the Buddha now goes, the brahmins and householders, and people of town and country will incline the same way. | T: Wherever the Blessed One will go now, the brahmans of the towns & countryside will be so inclined.
an6.42:3.4 | S: It’s like when the heavens rain heavily and the water flows downhill. | T: Just as when the rain-devas send rain in fat drops, the waters flow with the incline,
an6.42:3.5 | S: In the same way, wherever the Buddha now goes, the brahmins and householders, and people of town and country will incline the same way. | T: in the same way, wherever the Blessed One will go now, the brahmans of the towns & countryside will be so inclined.
an6.42:3.6 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an6.42:3.7 | S: Because of the Buddha’s ethics and wisdom.” | T: Because such is the Blessed One’s virtue & discernment.”
an6.42:4.1 | S: “Nāgita, may I never become famous. May fame not come to me. | T: “May I have nothing to do with honor, Nāgita, and honor nothing to do with me.
an6.42:4.2 | S: There are those who can’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: Whoever cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening,
an6.42:4.3 | S: Let them enjoy the filthy, lazy pleasure of possessions, honor, and popularity. | T: let him consent to this slimy-excrement-pleasure, this torpor-pleasure, this pleasure of gains, offerings, & fame.
an6.42:5.1 | S: Take a mendicant living within a village who I see sitting immersed in samādhi. | T: “Nāgita, there is the case where I see a monk sitting in concentration in a village dwelling.
an6.42:5.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an6.42:5.3 | S: ‘Now a monastery worker, a novice, or a fellow practitioner will make this venerable fall from immersion.’ | T: ‘Soon a monastery attendant will disturb this venerable one in some way, or a novice will, and rouse him from his concentration.’
an6.42:5.4 | S: So I’m not pleased that that mendicant is living within a village. | T: And so I am not pleased with that monk’s village-dwelling.
an6.42:6.1 | S: Take a mendicant in the wilderness who I see sitting nodding in meditation. | T: “But then there is the case where I see a monk sitting, nodding, in the wilderness.
an6.42:6.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an6.42:6.3 | S: ‘Now this venerable, having dispelled that sleepiness and weariness, will focus just on the unified perception of wilderness.’ | T: ‘Soon this venerable one will dispel his drowsiness & fatigue and attend to the wilderness-perception, (his mind) unified.’
an6.42:6.4 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.
an6.42:7.1 | S: Take a mendicant in the wilderness who I see sitting without being immersed in samādhi. | T: “Then there is the case where I see a wilderness monk sitting unconcentrated in the wilderness.
an6.42:7.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an6.42:7.3 | S: ‘Now if this venerable’s mind is not immersed in samādhi they will immerse it; or if it is immersed in samādhi, they will preserve it.’ | T: ‘Soon this venerable one will center his unconcentrated mind, or protect his concentrated mind.’
an6.42:7.4 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.
an6.42:8.1 | S: Take a mendicant in the wilderness who I see sitting immersed in samādhi. | T: “Then there is the case where I see a wilderness monk sitting in concentration in the wilderness.
an6.42:8.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an6.42:8.3 | S: ‘Now this venerable will free the unfreed mind or preserve the freed mind.’ | T: ‘Soon this venerable one will release his unreleased mind, or protect his released mind.’
an6.42:8.4 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.
an6.42:9.1 | S: Take a mendicant who I see living within a village receiving robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: “Then there is the case where I see a village-dwelling monk who receives robes, alms food, shelter, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick.
an6.42:9.2 | S: Enjoying possessions, honor, and popularity they neglect retreat, and they neglect remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest. | T: Receiving, as he likes, those gains, offerings, & fame, he neglects seclusion, he neglects isolated forest & wilderness dwellings.
an6.42:9.3 | S: They come down to villages, towns, and capital cities and make their home there. | T: He makes his living by visiting villages, towns, & cities.
an6.42:9.4 | S: So I’m not pleased that that mendicant is living within a village. | T: And so I am not pleased with that monk’s village-dwelling.
an6.42:10.1 | S: Take a mendicant who I see in the wilderness receiving robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: “Then there is the case where I see a wilderness monk who receives robes, alms food, shelter, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick.
an6.42:10.2 | S: Fending off possessions, honor, and popularity they don’t neglect retreat, and they don’t neglect remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest. | T: Fending off those gains, offerings, & fame, he doesn’t neglect seclusion, doesn’t neglect isolated forest & wilderness dwellings.
an6.42:10.3 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.

